It's the **name of the field to look for inside the ID token** that Okta sends back — not the groups themselves.

When someone signs in, Okta returns a JWT whose payload looks roughly like this:

```json
{
  "sub": "00u1a2b3c4d5e6f7g8h9",
  "email": "sarah@yourcompany.com",
  "name": "Sarah Chen",
  "groups": ["nao-admins", "engineering", "everyone"]
}
```

nao has to know which key holds the group list. `OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM=groups` says "read the `groups` key." Whatever array it finds there is then matched against your `OIDC_GROUP_ROLE_MAPPING` to decide the person's role.

## Why it's configurable at all

`groups` is the conventional name and the default, so with Okta you can usually leave it unset. It exists because providers don't agree on the name:

Auth0 requires custom claims to be namespaced with a URL, so you'd write `OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM=https://yourcompany.com/groups`. Keycloak lets you name the mapper whatever you like, and some teams use `roles`. Okta itself lets you pick the claim name when you create it — if someone on your team names it `nao_groups` in the Okta admin console, this variable is how you tell nao to look there instead.

Here's the line that consumes it, falling back to `groups`:

```62:68:apps/backend/src/services/sso-group-mapping.service.ts
async function readGroupsFromIdToken(userId: string): Promise<string[] | null> {
	const idToken = await accountQueries.getIdToken(userId, getOidcProviderId());
	if (!idToken) {
		return null;
	}

	return extractGroups(decodeJwt(idToken), env.OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM ?? DEFAULT_GROUPS_CLAIM);
```

## The failure mode to watch for

If this name doesn't match what Okta actually emits, nothing errors — nao just finds no groups, matches nothing, and leaves everyone on their existing role. That's the "Roles never change despite group mapping" row I added to the troubleshooting table. The tell is a `No mapped SSO group matched` line in the backend logs, which prints the groups nao actually saw so you can compare them against what you configured.

The other half of that same failure is the claim being emitted only on Okta's userinfo endpoint rather than in the ID token. nao only reads the ID token, so the claim has to be configured with "include in ID Token: Always" regardless of what you name it.

